@@s001 Happens-before: the rules
@@s002 dato:actions of the main,ora:t1,due:t2,no:no path
@@s003 <p>Each circle is an action of <code>PossibleReordering</code>. An arrow <code>a → b</code> means <b>a happens-before b</b>.</p><p>st1 = start(t1), jt1 = join(t1).</p>
@@s004 <p><b>Program order</b> of the main: each action of the main happens-before the next one.</p>
@@s005 <p>Program order of <b>t1</b> and of <b>t2</b>: inside each thread the order is that of the code.</p>
@@s006 <p><b>Thread start rule</b>: <code>start()</code> happens-before the <b>first</b> action of the started thread. Pairs: <code>m(start(t1)) → t1(1)</code> and <code>m(start(t2)) → t2(3)</code>.</p>
@@s007 <p><b>Thread termination rule</b>: the <b>last</b> action of a thread happens-before the <code>join()</code> on that thread. Pairs: <code>t1(2) → m(join(t1))</code> and <code>t2(4) → m(join(t2))</code>.</p>
@@s008 <p><b>Transitive rule</b>: if there is a path of arrows from a to b, then a happens-before b. For example <code>m(init(b)) → … → m(start(t1)) → t1(1) → t1(2)</code>: the initialisation of b is visible to <code>x=b</code>.</p>
@@s009 <p>And between <code>t1(1)</code> (writes a) and <code>t2(4)</code> (reads a)? Look for a path: from the actions of t1 one leaves only towards jt1, and from there one never returns to t2. And vice versa.</p><p><b>Neither pair</b> is in the order: the two actions are <b>not ordered</b> by happens-before. Accesses to the same variable, one of them a write: it is a <b>data race</b>.</p>
@@s010 dato:action,ok:pair in HB,due:pair by transitivity,no:not ordered
@@s011 dato:action a,due:action b,ok:a happens-before b
@@s012 dato:main,ora:t1,due:t2,ok:start and termination,no:missing pair
@@s013 Happens-before: the rules
@@s014 Program order orders the actions <b>inside</b> one thread. To know whether an action of one thread surely comes before an action of <b>another</b> thread (and so whether the second sees the effect of the first) the <b>happens-before</b> rules are needed.
@@s015 The pairs: what <code>a → b</code> means
@@s016 Definition
@@s017 Given two actions <code>a</code>, <code>b</code> of an execution, <b><code>a</code> happens-before <code>b</code></b> (written <code>a → b</code>) if the pair is obtained from one of the rules below. If <code>a → b</code>, the effect of <code>a</code> is <b>visible</b> to <code>b</code>.
@@s018 How the pairs are read
@@s019 (<mark class="dato">a</mark>, <mark class="ok">b</mark>) or <mark class="dato">a</mark> → <mark class="ok">b</mark>
@@s020 Same thing, two writes: a comes before b, guaranteed.
@@s021 A chain also contains the "skipped" pairs: transitivity holds.
@@s022 If neither pair is there, the actions are <b>not ordered</b>: they can happen in any order, and neither is guaranteed to see the effect of the other.
@@s023 "Partial": not all pairs of actions are comparable. The program order of a thread, instead, is <b>total</b>.
@@s024 Pairs, transitivity, unordered actions
@@s025 The eight rules
@@s026 Happens-before is not "felt": it is <b>built</b>, and only with these eight rules. A pair (a, b) is in the set only if a rule produces it. Three rules work <b>inside</b> a thread or before everything (program order, default init, final field init). Four link <b>different threads</b> and are the only ones that allow a thread to see the writes of another (monitor, volatile, start, termination). The last, the transitive one, glues the others into chains. If between two actions of different threads you cannot build a chain with these rules, those actions are not ordered.
@@s027 Rule
@@s028 a → b when…
@@s029 Example
@@s030 <b>Program order</b>
@@s031 a and b are of the same thread and a comes first in the code
@@s032 <b>Monitor</b>
@@s033 a is an <code>unlock()</code> and b a later <code>lock()</code> on the <b>same</b> monitor (lock, semaphore, barrier…)
@@s034 <code>t1(unlock) → t2(lock)</code>
@@s035 <b>Volatile</b>
@@s036 a is a write to a volatile variable and b a later read of the same variable
@@s037 <code>m(v=42) → t(read v)</code>
@@s038 <b>Default init</b>
@@s039 the default initialisation (0, false, null) comes before any other action
@@s040 field <code>int i;</code>
@@s041 <b>Final field init</b>
@@s042 the initialisation of the <code>final</code> (and static) fields comes before any other use, once the constructor has finished
@@s043 <code>final Object o</code>
@@s044 <b>Thread start</b>
@@s045 a is <code>start()</code> and b is the <b>first</b> action of the started thread
@@s046 <code>m(start(t1)) → t1(1)</code>
@@s047 <b>Thread termination</b>
@@s048 a is the <b>last</b> action of a thread and b the <code>join()</code> on that thread, from any other thread
@@s049 <code>t1(2) → m(join(t1))</code>
@@s050 <b>Transitive</b>
@@s051 chains
@@s052 The eight rules, one at a time
@@s053 The graph, rule by rule
@@s054 Happens-before in PossibleReordering
@@s055 Question from the slide: which pairs give start and termination?
@@s056 Start rule: <code>m(start(t1)) → t1(1)</code>, <code>m(start(t2)) → t2(3)</code>. Termination rule: <code>t1(2) → m(join(t1))</code>, <code>t2(4) → m(join(t2))</code>. Careful: the start rule links <code>start</code> only to the <b>first</b> action of the thread, the termination rule only the <b>last</b>. The others follow by transitivity with the program order.
@@s057 Building the sets as in the slides
@@s058 At the exam the graph is written as <b>sets of pairs</b>: one for each rule that applies (POm, PO1, PO2 for the program order, S for start, T for termination, then possibly M for the monitor and V for volatile). The happens-before is the <b>transitive closure</b> of the union, indicated with ⁺: one adds all the pairs that follow from a chain. To decide whether two actions are ordered it is enough to look for a path in the graph, in one direction or the other.
@@s059 The sets of pairs of PossibleReordering (slide 27)
@@s060 Program order of the main.
@@s061 Program order of the threads.
@@s062 Pairs of the start rule.
@@s063 Pairs of the termination rule.
@@s064 HB = transitive closure of ( PO<sub>m</sub> ∪ PO<sub>1</sub> ∪ PO<sub>2</sub> ∪ S ∪ T )
@@s065 The complete happens-before: all the pairs, plus those that follow by transitivity. Here it is the same for <b>all</b> the executions, because there is only one possible order for start and join.
@@s066 No pair links actions of t1 to actions of t2 (except going through the main in the wrong direction). Conclusion: data race between <code>t1(1)</code> and <code>t2(4)</code> in <b>every</b> execution.
@@s067 From POm, PO1, PO2, S, T to HB
